{
  "id": "6-10-breakout-5-lab-bounce",
  "title": "6.10.5 Lab: Bounce",
  "description": "The ball moves by two speeds you control. Bounce it off three walls and the paddle, let the paddle steer it, and serve it again when it falls out.",
  "type": "lesson",
  "estimateMins": 20,
  "category": "Unit 2: moSHion: Applied Game Development",
  "unit": "6.10 Breakout",
  "preview": "moshion",
  "week": 18,
  "slos": [
    "SLO-3",
    "SLO-4"
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "title": "Move the ball",
      "instructions": "The starter has the finished paddle from 6.10.4 and a ball: a white circle 12 pixels wide, so its radius `R` is 6. It has `collider = 'none'`, so nothing pushes it, slows it or pulls it down: your code moves it, as with Defender's shots in 5.9.5. `vx` and `vy` hold its speed in pixels per frame: `vx` across (`+` is right) and `vy` down (`+` is down, `-` is up). `serve()` puts the ball at (200, 300) with `vx` 3 and `vy` -4, and `draw()` calls `moveBall()` every frame, which is empty. The words at the top show vx and vy.\n\nIn `moveBall()`, add `vx` to the ball's x and `vy` to its y. Run it: the ball flies up and to the right, and off the screen. Press Stop, then Run, to bring it back.",
      "hints": [
        "`ball.x += vx;` and `ball.y += vy;`",
        "`+=` adds to what is there: `ball.x += vx` is `ball.x = ball.x + vx`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "title": "Bounce off three walls",
      "instructions": "A bounce is a flipped sign: `vx = -vx` turns 3 into -3 (going left) and -3 back into 3. The ball's x is its centre, so its side touches the left wall when `ball.x` is `R`, not 0, and the right wall when it is `400 - R` (6.10.2). Under the two moving lines, add: if `ball.x` is less than `R` and `vx` is below 0 (moving left), flip `vx`; if `ball.x` is more than `400 - R` and `vx` is above 0 (moving right), flip `vx`. A wall turns only a ball that is moving into it, the same idea as the paddle test you add next: the paddle can steer a ball back toward a wall it is still touching, and without the direction test that wall would flip it on every frame and glue it there (6.10.2). Then: if `ball.y` is less than `R` (the top), flip `vy`. There is no bottom wall. Run it: the ball bounces off the left, the right and the top, and falls out at the bottom.",
      "hints": [
        "`if (ball.x < R && vx < 0) vx = -vx;` and `if (ball.x > 400 - R && vx > 0) vx = -vx;`",
        "`if (ball.y < R) vy = -vy;`"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "title": "Off the paddle, and steer",
      "instructions": "`ball.overlaps(paddle)` is true on every frame the two touch (5.9.5), so flip `vy` only while the ball is coming down: `if (ball.overlaps(paddle) && vy > 0) { ... }`. Inside, flip `vy`, then set `vx` from where the ball landed: `vx = (ball.x - paddle.x) / 10;`. `ball.x - paddle.x` is how far right of the paddle's centre the ball is, negative on the left half (the A-to-B subtraction of 6.8.8), so the left end sends the ball left and the right end sends it right. Run it and catch the ball on different parts of the paddle: watch `vx` change in the words.",
      "hints": [
        "Two lines inside the `if`: `vy = -vy;` and then `vx = (ball.x - paddle.x) / 10;`.",
        "Without `&& vy > 0`, a paddle sliding into the ball from the side can flip it again and again while they touch."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "title": "A lost ball comes back",
      "instructions": "The bottom is open. At the end of `moveBall()`, if the ball's y is more than `400 + R` (all of it below the bottom edge), call `serve()` to put it back. Run it, miss on purpose, and the ball comes back above the paddle.",
      "hints": [
        "`if (ball.y > 400 + R) serve();`",
        "`serve()` is already written: it puts the ball back and resets vx and vy."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "requirements": [
    {
